Pay secrecy bans have been suggested as a solution to wage gaps. In this paper, we find evidence that these policies lead to a short-term increase in pay compression, and that they lead to an increase in lawsuits. Using Current Population Survey (CPS) data, we exploit the variation in US states that passed these laws between 1977 and 2021. We find evidence of a short-run compression in the dispersion of managers’ wages in line with previous literature. We also find that discrimination complaints (lawsuits) filed with the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission (EEOC) between 2009 and 2021 increased by 25-40%

